.TH WERKMEISTER-FEATURE-UP 1 "" "werkmeister" "Werkmeister Manual"
.SH NAME
werkmeister\-feature\-up \- Detect services, allocate slot, generate config, start containers
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwerkmeister feature up\fR [feature]
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-w, \-\-workspace <path>\fR
Path to directory containing repo checkouts. Default: ~/workspace (override with paths.workspaces_root in ~/.werkmeister/config.yaml).
.TP
\fB\-\-dev\fR
Run the file watchers in cli/library/extension containers, which otherwise idle.
.TP
\fB\-\-service <name>\fR
Start only this service and wait for it, the way the PR gate does — including one behind the on\-demand profile. \fBfeature stop\fR stops it.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The local services come from the workspace's \fB.werkmeister/workspace.yml\fR (via \fBwerkmeister workspace init\fR), not from a flag.
.PP
\fBWhat it does:\fR
.IP 1. 3
Loads the service registry and port configuration, and stops with a clear message if a secret one of the workspace's services needs is missing
.IP 2. 3
Resolves the workspace (\fB\-\-workspace\fR, else \fBpaths.workspaces_root\fR from the config, else the nearest enclosing repo's parent, else \fB~/workspace/\fR) and reads its local services from the \fBSERVICES=\fR line \fBwerkmeister workspace init\fR wrote to the workspace \fB.env\fR
.IP 3. 3
Allocates a slot (1–12) if the feature doesn't already have one
.IP 4. 3
When the workspace has local services, checks the host's memory and CPU budget (\fBwerkmeister budget status\fR). If the feature doesn't fit it waits in a queue and starts when another feature stops. A start that fits goes ahead of a queue head that doesn't, unless that head is a gate's start or has waited 5 minutes. A queued whole\-feature head that would fit holds nothing: only another feature stopping starts it. The queue head may stop features whose session is idle or waiting on a person (a question or plan; a permission prompt is mid\-turn and is not), oldest first, and says which: a service start at once, a plain \fBfeature up\fR after 5 minutes. It never stops a feature started in the last 5 minutes, whose session may not have had its first turn yet; only a person's start can. A feature a person starts may also stop working autonomous ones (\fBbudget.preempt: false\fR turns all of it off). A \fB\-\-service\fR start that has to wait while an autonomous workspace's feature already runs containers stops them first, so it holds nothing while it waits, then starts them again with the new service
.IP 5. 3
Generates per\-feature files in the feature's workspace:
.IP \(bu 6
\fBcompose.yml\fR — Docker Compose stack definition (workspace root)
.IP \(bu 6
\fB.env\fR — Environment variables with resolved service URLs (workspace root)
.IP \(bu 6
\fB<workspace>/.werkmeister/supergraph\-config.yml\fR — GraphQL federation config (only when a service has a GraphQL type: \fBgraphql\-subgraph\fR, \fBgraphql\fR, \fBsubgraph\fR or \fBfederation\fR)
.IP 6. 3
Verifies k8s port\-forwards are healthy (restarts if needed), only when a local service needs the cluster
.IP 7. 3
Starts containers with \fBdocker compose up \-d \-\-build \-\-remove\-orphans\fR
.IP 8. 3
Regenerates \fB~/.werkmeister/infra/proxy/Caddyfile\fR and reloads Caddy (or starts it, when something here is routed)
.IP 9. 3
Warns about routed hostnames that do not resolve to 127.0.0.1 on this machine and prints the \fB/etc/hosts\fR line to add (the command still succeeds)
.PP
\fBOutput example:\fR
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
=== werkmeister up: my\-feature ===

[info] Loading configuration...
[ok]   Allocated slot 1 for 'my\-feature'

  Local services: backend, frontend

[info] Checking k8s port\-forwards...
[info] Starting containers...
[info] Configuring reverse proxy...

=== Feature 'my\-feature' is ready ===

  URL:     https://frontend.local.werkmeister.io
  Cookie:  X\-Feature=my\-feature
  Slot:    1 (ports 11100\-11119)

  Local services:
    * backend \-> localhost:11100
    * frontend \-> localhost:11101

  k8s services: (everything not listed above)

  Useful commands:
    werkmeister feature status            # view all features
    werkmeister feature logs my\-feature    # tail logs
    werkmeister feature test my\-feature    # run Playwright tests
    werkmeister feature down my\-feature    # tear down
.RE
.fi
.PP
\fBIf the workspace lists no services\fR, the command still proceeds — the feature is registered and routes entirely to k8s. To add services later, list them in \fB.werkmeister/workspace.yml\fR, rerun \fBwerkmeister workspace init\fR, then \fBwerkmeister feature up\fR.
.PP
\fBIf the feature has to wait for capacity\fR, the command exits 75 without starting containers. It prints \fB=== Feature '<name>' is waiting for capacity (position N) ===\fR, who holds the budget, what the start needs against what is free, and the head it waits behind. A holder whose session waits on a person with a run in flight is named with the step that frees it: answer its question, or stop it from the pane. The feature stays queued and starts by itself when room frees, and its session is told to continue. A \fB\-\-service\fR start waits up to 10 minutes (at once when only such holders could make room), then exits 75 the same way and stays queued: it starts by itself when room frees, and a rerun keeps its place. One that stopped the feature's running containers to wait does not stay queued: \fBwerkmeister feature up\fR starts the feature again. A \fB\-\-service\fR start while the whole feature waits replaces that wait: the feature leaves the queue for its whole start and starts just that service. Exit 75 means no capacity yet, not a failure. \fBwerkmeister budget status\fR shows the queue.
.PP
\fBWhat a queued session can do.\fR While its whole feature waits (\fB.werkmeister/waiting.json\fR), the werkmeister hook denies the session's commands that need its containers or the CPUs: those that run \fBdocker\fR, \fBpnpm\fR, \fBnpm\fR, \fBnpx\fR, \fBnode\fR, \fBturbo\fR, \fBvitest\fR, \fBwith\-temp\-home\fR, \fBwith\-fixture\-workspace\fR, \fBwerkmeister feature restart\fR, \fBpr gate\fR or \fBpr open\fR as a program (in any \fB&&\fR, \fB;\fR or \fB|\fR step, \fB$(…)\fR, or \fBsh \-c\fR script), and their MCP forms. Everything else passes: edits, git, \fBwerkmeister feature stop\fR, \fBwerkmeister budget status\fR, \fBdocker ps\fR, a commit message, \fBprintf\fR, \fBsed\fR or heredoc that only mentions those names, and \fBwerkmeister feature up\fR, which only asks admission again (exit 75 while there is no room) and so starts a queued feature once room frees, for example after \fBbudget:\fR grows. The deny does not end the turn, so the session goes on with other work. Before it denies, the hook asks Docker whether the feature's containers already run; if they do, the queue entry is stale, and it removes \fBwaiting.json\fR and lets the command through. When a grant starts the feature, the session is told \fBCapacity freed: … Continue where you left off.\fR: the workspaces pane sends that to the workspace's Desktop session, and keeps it for its next refresh when the send fails. A \fB\-\-service\fR start's own wait holds nothing, since its poll is the session's own command. A paused workspace holds every tool and ends the turn.
.PP
\fBIf compose gives up with a container left Created\fR (its 20\-minute limit ran out under load, and the container was created but never started), \fBup\fR starts that container itself, warns \fBdocker compose gave up with <name> left Created; started it directly\fR, and carries on. It doesn't when another of the feature's containers failed (exited non\-zero or unhealthy), since compose holds its dependents back on purpose: then the compose failure stands. A container that still won't start fails the command, naming it and the \fBdocker start <name>\fR that starts it by hand.
.PP
\fBIf every slot is taken\fR (12 by default, \fBfeature_slots.count\fR), the command exits with an error. Free a slot by running \fBwerkmeister feature down\fR on another feature.
.PP
\fB`\-\-service <name>`\fR starts only that one service, the way the PR gate starts it, and prints \fBStarted <feature>\-<name>\fR.
.PP
\fBAdding a service to a running feature.\fR To run another service locally, add it to the \fBservices\fR field in the workspace's \fB.werkmeister/workspace.yml\fR, rerun \fBwerkmeister workspace init\fR (this also adds the owning repo's worktree if it isn't there yet), then \fBwerkmeister feature up\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBwerkmeister\fR(1)
